# ---- per-limb falsifiers (13 reviewers): independent re-derivation + second mutant
for lid, desc in LIMBS:
    it(W2, "ExecLimb" + lid.replace("-", ""), "executor", """Falsify ledger limb `%s` (%s) of the C1 ledger on the LANDED bytes. Wave-1 landed the counter {LANE}/corpus/instruments/limb-mutation-check.py and ledger {LANE}/corpus/instruments/limb-ledger.json (read-only for you; all work in a mktemp scratch copy of {LANE}/corpus). (1) Re-derive by hand: read the ledger row id `%s`, copy {LANE}/corpus to a scratch dir, run the row's kill command on the UNMUTATED copy (record exit + whether `signal` matches), apply the row's find/replace yourself with python (assert find occurs exactly once; `python3 -m py_compile` the result), run the kill command again, and compare with the counter's `LIMB %s ...` line from `python3 corpus/instruments/limb-mutation-check.py --ledger corpus/instruments/limb-ledger.json` (run it in the lane; it is scratch-only). Any difference between your hand result and the counter's line is a defect. (2) Discrimination: design a SECOND, different one-limb mutant of the same limb (different code site or inversion) and run the kill command - say whether it is killed; if the instrument's own cases kill only one specific mutant shape, say so. (3) Isolation: show the limb's mutant is killed by a case that is specific to this limb (list which output lines differ), not by an unrelated crash: the mutant run must still print the instrument's normal banner/other cases. Write {WD}/ExecLimb%s.md with commands, outputs and a verdict `holds|weak|defect`.""" % (lid, desc, lid, lid, lid.replace("-", "")),
       )

# ---- counter mutants (6 mutation-testers)
CMUT = [
    ("MutCtrAlwaysKilled", "skip the mutant run and report killed for every valid row (always-killed)"),
    ("MutCtrNoLiveBaseline", "skip the live-baseline run (never produce invalid:live-baseline; compare mutant only to the declared live_exit)"),
    ("MutCtrNoCompileCheck", "drop the py_compile check so a mutant that does not compile counts as killed"),
    ("MutCtrNonUniqueFind", "accept a find text that occurs more than once (apply to the first occurrence) instead of invalid:find-count"),
    ("MutCtrSignalIgnored", "ignore the `signal` regex (killed only by exit-code difference)"),
    ("MutCtrTimeoutKilled", "treat a kill-command timeout as killed instead of invalid:timeout"),
]
for name, desc in CMUT:
    it(W2, name, "mutation-tester", """Counter mutant attack: in a scratch copy (mktemp; never edit {LANE}) of {LANE}/corpus/instruments/limb-mutation-check.py apply this one mutation: %s. Run the mutant counter against {LANE}/corpus/instruments/limb-ledger.json (copy the ledger and the corpus tree into the scratch lane and use `--root`). The frozen C1 design (claims.json `mutants_of_the_counter`) requires each counter mutant to fail the 3 controls or the LIMB-line clause, i.e. NOT print both `limbs=13 killed=13 survivors=0` and `controls=3 rejected=3` with exit 0. Report: the mutant diff, the mutant's full output and exit code, whether it is KILLED (output differs from the real counter in a way the frozen kill rule catches - name the clause or control) or a SURVIVOR (identical to the real run - a hole in the counter). If it survives, propose the smallest extra control/ledger row and message ExecCounterFix (write agent://ExecCounterFix) with the mutant diff. Also run the REAL counter twice and confirm byte-identical output. Write {WD}/%s.md.""" % (desc, name))

# ---- C1 integration / no-harm
